> How many new players
> actually set up a farm (using, say, pudding kill counts as a

This is the reason that a number of suggestions for "fix farming" fail;
what counts as a farm?  Ten kills?  One hundred?  Some people farm
briefly for intrinsics and then move on -- is this bad?  It certainly
isn't on the level of "farm for MAXINT-1 score" that ctaboir used,
that's for sure.

>> [snip] When the capacity
>> exists to obtain infinite items, the game itself reduced from
>> multi-dimensional to one-dimensional, and this is _never_ good.
> 
> And that's why playing in wizard mode isn't any fun. But even pudding
> farming is not one-dimensional, or all farmers would always ascend and
> no-one would be annoyed by stepping into puddingopolis bones files.

Pudding farming is, in fact, one-dimensional and reduces the game to the
same after the farmer is concluded; he simply uses all his wands of
wishing, full healing potions, scrolls of charging, etc. and ascends.

The conclusions that "all farmers would ascend, therefore pudding
farming is not broken" is relying on player incompetence as the core of
the argument; competent farmers, however, simply do not die (except
intentionally).

Assuming that players will be careless is not an effective way to design
a game -- then you make the game broken for anyone who is willing to not
be careless.

> My personal opinion is that a game of the complexity of Nethack, in
> which every single decision requires mulling through dozens of
> possible pros and cons, would be far too tedious. There are so many
> other things in Nethack that require careful judgment and compromise
> that having a few clear "best-choice" cases is, on the whole, a
> benefit.

Which things exactly do you think require careful judgement and
compromise in vanilla currently?  By and large, the entire mid- to late-
game is a no-brainer right now; if there weren't early-game tactical
decisions to make (ie. BEFORE you have all your resources lined up in a
row), it would be boring beyond belief.

In fact, some of the anti-farmers who post here have openly admitted
that they don't even play the game to the end anymore because 'it's
boring'... which proves my point most efficiently.  If there were enough
things that required some judgement and compromise, it wouldn't be
boring at all.

